    WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or
    implied. See the License for the specific language governing
    permissions and limitations under the License.

Source: <https://github.com/adobe-type-tools/agl-aglfn>. MODIFICATION:
the table was narrowed to the 448 single-scalar names whose codepoint a
1-byte simple font can reach (Latin U+0020–U+017F, punctuation and
currency U+2000–U+20BF, U+2122, and the Latin ligatures U+FB00–U+FB06)
and re-packed as whitespace-separated `name HEX` pairs. It is used to
decode a PDF simple font's `/Encoding /Differences` glyph names to text.

Test fixtures
-------------

The mscx and reference MIDI files under `Tests/SheetMusicTests/Resources/`
are copies of test fixtures from the upstream MuseScore repository
(`src/importexport/midi/tests/midiexport_data/` in
<https://github.com/musescore/MuseScore>), used here to verify
byte/semantic equivalence with MuseScore's own MIDI exporter output.
These files are licensed under GPL-3.0; see
`Tests/SheetMusicTests/Resources/LICENSE`.

That directory also holds a small number of fixtures hand-authored for
this project, which are MIT-licensed rather than GPL-3.0. They are
listed by name under "MIT-licensed fixtures" in
`Tests/SheetMusicTests/Resources/LICENSE`, which is authoritative for
the per-file split.

Because these fixtures are confined to the test target and are not
distributed as part of the published library products, they do not affect
the licence of code that depends on this package.

Third-party Swift dependencies
------------------------------

Resolved via Swift Package Manager (all Apache-2.0 licensed):

- swift-java — Apache 2.0 (https://github.com/swiftlang/swift-java)
- swift-subprocess — Apache 2.0 (https://github.com/swiftlang/swift-subprocess)
- swift-wirelet — Apache 2.0 (https://github.com/jiyimeta/swift-wirelet)

ZIP handling is implemented in-house (`Sources/SheetMusicZip`); no
third-party ZIP library is used. Its DEFLATE backend is Apple's
`Compression` framework on Apple platforms and `zlib` elsewhere.

Vendored zlib (WebAssembly builds only)
---------------------------------------

Linux and Android link the system `zlib`. The WebAssembly SDK ships none,
so `Sources/zlib` carries the raw-DEFLATE subset of zlib 1.3.1 by
Jean-loup Gailly and Mark Adler, unmodified, under the zlib License —
see `Sources/zlib/LICENSE` for the full text and
`Sources/zlib/README.md` for what was taken and why.

That target is added to the package **only when
`SWIFT_SHEET_MUSIC_WASM=1` is exported**, so it is absent from the Apple
and Android builds and from the published package shape. The zlib License
is permissive and imposes no reciprocal obligation on code that links it;
it asks only that the copyright notice be retained, which vendoring the
sources verbatim does.

